  <article class="wrap prose-page">
    <p class="label">Colophon</p>
    <h1>How this site was built</h1>

    <p>
      I built this site with <a href="https://claude.com/claude-code">Claude Code</a>, Anthropic’s coding agent. I
      want to be specific about what that means, because “built with AI” has started to mean everything from “I typed
      one prompt” to nothing at all.
    </p>

    <h2>What I did</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Decided what the site is for and who it’s for: people hiring a VP of Engineering.</li>
      <li>
        Answered a long interview about my career, my projects and what I would and wouldn’t say publicly. Claude
        asked; I decided.
      </li>
      <li>
        Chose the stack (Astro, S3, CloudFront) and the concept. The first version Claude built was a résumé in
        disguise; I threw it out and asked for a showcase.
      </li>
      <li>Decided every piece of content that stayed.</li>
      <li>Reviewed every page, rejected plenty of it, and edited the copy until it sounded like me.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What Claude did</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Read my old portfolio, my LinkedIn, my GitHub and the sites for my projects, and pointed out what was missing
        and what didn’t hold up.
      </li>
      <li>Wrote the code: the Astro components, the styles and the deploy setup.</li>
      <li>Drafted the copy from my own material, for me to cut and rewrite.</li>
      <li>Checked its own work in a browser, at desktop and phone widths, in light and dark mode.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why say so</h2>
    <p>
      Because it’s true, and because it’s the job now. An engineering leader in 2026 should know first-hand what these
      tools are good at, where they go wrong, and how much judgment they still need. The same goes for the
      <a href="/#ai">projects on the home page</a>: each one says who did what.
    </p>

    <p>The source is on <a href="https://github.com/arsindelve/michaellane.ai">GitHub</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>

